## Why Java for the backend?

You already know how a web application works from the frontend side: React renders the interface, TypeScript describes data and functions, and the browser sends HTTP requests to a backend. In this course, the backend will be written in Java with Spring Boot.

Java is a statically typed, object-oriented programming language. **Statically typed** means that types are checked by the compiler before your program runs. Java also has a large ecosystem for building web applications, and Spring Boot provides the framework we will use for HTTP APIs.

If you come from TypeScript, you already understand many of the ideas. The biggest difference is that Java makes much more of the structure explicit.

## Install JDK 21

The **JDK (Java Development Kit)** contains the tools required to develop and compile Java applications. We will use Java 21, an LTS (Long-Term Support) version of Java.

Install a JDK 21 distribution such as Eclipse Temurin or another OpenJDK distribution. After installation, check that Java is available from your terminal:

~~~shell
java -version
~~~

You should see Java 21 in the output.

You can find the official Java documentation on [Oracle's Java documentation](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/).

## Create a project in IntelliJ IDEA Community

Our course IDE is IntelliJ IDEA Community Edition. You do not need IntelliJ's paid edition for the Java work in this course.

~~~callout ide Create the project
**File → New → Project → Java**, choose **Maven** as the build system and **JDK 21**. Run `Main` with the green ▶ in the gutter (**Shift+F10**). **Alt+Enter** on a red word offers the fix — usually a missing import.
~~~

Create a new Java project and select JDK 21 as the project SDK. IntelliJ can compile and run ordinary Java applications directly.

For the Spring Boot projects later in this module, we will use Maven. Maven is a build tool that downloads dependencies, compiles the application, runs tests, and packages the application.

For now, a simple Java project is enough. You can run a class containing a `main` method directly from IntelliJ.

~~~java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}
~~~

The `main` method is the entry point of a normal Java application.

## Java types vs TypeScript types

The first major difference you will notice is how explicit Java is about types.

~~~compare
{"left": {"title": "TypeScript", "lang": "ts", "code": "interface Task {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  done?: boolean;\n}\nconst tasks: Task[] = [];", "note": "Types are erased before the code runs."}, "right": {"title": "Java", "lang": "java", "code": "record Task(\n    long id,\n    String title,\n    boolean done) {}\n\nList<Task> tasks = new ArrayList<>();", "note": "Types are enforced by the compiler and at runtime."}}
~~~

In TypeScript you might write:

~~~typescript
const name: string = "Anna";
const age: number = 31;
~~~

Java looks similar, but the type comes before the variable name:

~~~java
String name = "Anna";
int age = 31;
~~~

Java has primitive types such as `int`, `long`, `double`, and `boolean`, as well as reference types such as `String` and your own classes.

TypeScript is also statically typed during development, but Java's type system is part of the compiled Java program. Java does not simply remove type annotations before running the resulting source as TypeScript does.

| TypeScript | Java | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `string` | `String` | Text |
| `number` | `int`, `long`, `double` | Numbers |
| `boolean` | `boolean` | True/false |
| `T[]` | `T[]` or `List<T>` | Collection of values |
| `Record<string, number>` | `Map<String, Integer>` | Key/value data |
| `interface User` | `interface User` | Contract |
| `class User` | `class User` | Object/class definition |

One important detail: Java has primitive types such as `int`, while generic collections require reference types. Therefore you normally use `Integer` instead of `int` inside `List<Integer>` or `Map<String, Integer>`.

## Classes, fields and constructors

A Java class defines the structure and behaviour of an object.

In TypeScript:

~~~typescript
class Task {
    constructor(public title: string, public completed: boolean) {}
}
~~~

A similar Java class is more explicit:

~~~java
public class Task {
    private String title;
    private boolean completed;

    public Task(String title, boolean completed) {
        this.title = title;
        this.completed = completed;
    }
}
~~~

A **field** is a variable stored inside an object. A **constructor** is the special method used when an object is created.

~~~java
Task task = new Task("Learn Java", false);
~~~

The `new` keyword creates the object.

## Access modifiers

Java gives you explicit access modifiers. The most common ones are `public`, `private`, and `protected`.

~~~java
public class Task {
    private String title;

    public String getTitle() {
        return title;
    }
}
~~~

`private` means the field can only be accessed directly inside the class. `public` allows access from other classes. This is useful for controlling how an object can be changed.

TypeScript also has `public`, `private`, and `protected`, so the concept should already be familiar.

## Interfaces

An interface describes a contract: it says which methods or properties a type must provide.

TypeScript:

~~~typescript
interface PaymentService {
    pay(amount: number): void;
}
~~~

Java:

~~~java
public interface PaymentService {
    void pay(double amount);
}
~~~

A class implements the interface with `implements`:

~~~java
public class CardPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    @Override
    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid: " + amount);
    }
}
~~~

`@Override` tells the compiler that the method is implementing a method defined by the interface or inherited from a parent class. The compiler can then catch mistakes such as a misspelled method name.

## Records for immutable data

Java has a special type called a **record** for representing simple data that should not normally be changed after creation.

For example, a task response could be:

~~~java
public record TaskResponse(Long id, String title, boolean completed) {}
~~~

You can create one like this:

~~~java
TaskResponse response = new TaskResponse(1L, "Learn Java", false);
~~~

The record automatically provides accessors, so you can write `response.title()` rather than `response.getTitle()`.

Records are particularly useful for API request and response data. We will use them more in the REST API lessons.

The important idea is that a record describes data rather than a mutable object with lots of behaviour.

## Strings and text blocks

Java uses `String` for text:

~~~java
String message = "Hello, developer!";
~~~

Java also supports text blocks, which are useful for multiline text:

~~~java
String json = """
    {
      "name": "Anna",
      "active": true
    }
    """;
~~~

This is especially convenient when working with SQL, JSON examples, or other multiline content.

Strings are immutable in Java. Operations such as concatenation produce a new string rather than changing the original string.

## Collections and generics

The three collections you will use constantly in backend development are `List`, `Map`, and `Set`.

~~~java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
Map<String, Integer> quantities = new HashMap<>();
Set<String> roles = new HashSet<>();
~~~

A `List` is an ordered collection and can contain duplicates. A `Map` stores key/value pairs. A `Set` stores unique values.

The `<String>` and `<String, Integer>` parts are **generics**. Generics tell Java which types a collection is allowed to contain.

For example:

~~~java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("Anna");
names.add("David");

String first = names.get(0);
~~~

This gives you compiler-checked type safety. You cannot accidentally add an integer to `List<String>`.

## null and Optional

Java has `null`, which represents the absence of a reference value.

~~~java
String email = null;
~~~

Using a null value incorrectly can cause a `NullPointerException` at runtime.

Java also provides `Optional<T>`, which represents a value that may or may not exist.

~~~java
Optional<String> email = Optional.of("anna@example.com");
~~~

An empty value can be represented with:

~~~java
Optional<String> email = Optional.empty();
~~~

You will frequently encounter `Optional` when working with Spring Data repositories. It is especially useful for expressing that a database lookup might not find anything.

Do not treat `Optional` as a replacement for every nullable field. Its most common use in our backend code will be for return values where the result may not exist.

## Streams: map, filter and collect

Java streams provide a way to process collections using operations similar to array methods in JavaScript and TypeScript.

~~~diagram
{"type": "flow", "title": "A stream pipeline", "nodes": [{"id": "l", "label": "tasks", "sub": "List<Task>", "kind": "code"}, {"id": "s", "label": ".stream()"}, {"id": "f", "label": ".filter(t -> !t.done())"}, {"id": "m", "label": ".map(Task::title)"}, {"id": "c", "label": ".toList()", "sub": "List<String>", "highlight": true}], "edges": [{"from": "l", "to": "s"}, {"from": "s", "to": "f"}, {"from": "f", "to": "m"}, {"from": "m", "to": "c"}]}
~~~

TypeScript:

~~~typescript
const activeNames = users
    .filter(user => user.active)
    .map(user => user.name);
~~~

Java:

~~~java
List<String> activeNames = users.stream()
    .filter(user -> user.isActive())
    .map(User::getName)
    .toList();
~~~

`filter` keeps matching elements. `map` transforms each element. `toList()` collects the result into a list.

This style is common in Java backend code, but do not force streams into every situation. A normal loop can sometimes be easier to understand.

## Exceptions and error handling

Java uses **exceptions** to represent errors and unusual situations.

~~~java
try {
    processPayment();
} catch (PaymentException exception) {
    System.out.println(exception.getMessage());
}
~~~

Java distinguishes between checked and unchecked exceptions.

A **checked exception** must be handled or declared by the method. An **unchecked exception** extends `RuntimeException` and does not have that requirement.

For example:

~~~java
public void loadFile() throws IOException {
    // operation that can throw IOException
}
~~~

`IOException` is a checked exception. `IllegalArgumentException` is an example of an unchecked exception.

In Spring applications, you will commonly use unchecked exceptions for invalid application state or invalid arguments and let Spring's web layer translate appropriate exceptions into HTTP responses. We will cover that properly in the validation and error-handling lesson.

## Packages and naming conventions

Java projects organize classes into **packages**, which are similar to namespaces.

~~~java
package hu.softwaredevelopment.taskmanager.task;
~~~

A class in that package might be:

~~~java
package hu.softwaredevelopment.taskmanager.task;

public class TaskService {
}
~~~

Packages normally use lowercase names. Classes use `PascalCase`, methods and variables use `camelCase`, and constants commonly use `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`.

~~~java
public class TaskService {
    private static final int MAX_TITLE_LENGTH = 200;

    public String createTask(String title) {
        return title.trim();
    }
}
~~~

As the course grows, packages will become important because our task manager will contain controllers, services, repositories, entities, and other classes.

## Common mistakes

### Treating Java like JavaScript

JavaScript allows a lot of dynamic behaviour. Java expects you to be explicit. Do not try to avoid types or structure; the compiler is there to help you catch mistakes early.

### Using `==` for String content

For object values such as strings, use `.equals()` when comparing content:

~~~java
if (name.equals("David")) {
    // ...
}
~~~

`==` compares object references rather than string content in the way you usually want.

### Making every field public

Public fields make it difficult to control how objects are modified. Prefer encapsulation with private fields and appropriate methods or constructors.

### Overusing Optional

`Optional` is useful, but it is not a general-purpose replacement for every nullable value. Use it where it communicates that a result may be absent.

### Writing streams that are harder to read than loops

Streams are powerful, but readability matters more than showing off functional syntax. If a straightforward loop is clearer, use the loop.

### Putting everything into one class

A backend quickly becomes difficult to maintain if controllers, business logic, and database operations all live together. Later in this module, we will separate these responsibilities into layers.

## What comes next?

You now have the Java foundation needed for Spring Boot. The next lesson will create the actual Spring Boot project with Maven and show how the application starts, how dependencies work, and how IntelliJ IDEA Community runs the project.

The goal is not to memorise every Java feature before continuing. You will learn the language while using it to build the task manager, just as you learned TypeScript by building real frontend features.

